PUBLIC RESPONSE: I concur with the unanimous assessment from round 1.

D is the incorrect statement.

* Xiapei 下帔/霞帔 is an archaic Zhou–Han ritual shoulder sash/mantle used in liyi and nashou marriage rites as a rank symbol. It was already in decline by the Song and never became the everyday “standard attire for married women” after the Song. Post-Song married women’s daily/formal dress is the ruqun / qun-kua / aoqun shirt-and-skirt ensemble. In Ming-Qing the xiapei survives only as part of the bridal ceremonial set fengguan-xiapei, worn for the wedding rite, not as standard married-women’s dress.

* The second clause conflates distinct headpieces and gets the chronology wrong. The floral/wreath crowns huaguan / hua guan / mu guan of Han–Wei–Tang early ceremonial wear are a different class of ornament from the fengguan. The fengguan 凤冠 is the elaborate metal-wire, jewel-set ceremonial headdress with phoenix and bird motifs codified for imperial consorts and elite brides in the Ming and onward, not a “floral crown” and not a Song-and-after development of xiapei-based dress.

A, B, C and E remain essentially correct in broad terms:
A. Ming codification of the Han wedding canon: red xiu he fu / dragon-phoenix robe, fengguan and hongsa red veil;
B. Zhou Li Six Rites: bride departs natal home with head covered by sa/mian thin silk veil;
C. Tang–Song shan mian: bride shields face with folding fan/handkerchief on departure;
E. Qing Han Chinese brides retain Ming form: red silk inner blouse under red embroidered robe, wide red skirt, red silk shoes embroidered with phoenix under fengguan.

Hence the false statement is D.

